<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">State of Pasundan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Negara Pasundan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The State of Pasundan was a federal state created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in 1948, named after the Sundanese homeland, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a>. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in December 1949, but it was widely seen as a Dutch creation. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-westerling">Raymond Westerling</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> coup attempt in Bandung in January 1950 discredited the federal order, it was dissolved into the Republic of Indonesia in early 1950.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/state-of-pasundan.jpg" alt="State of Pasundan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pasundan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a> on 17 August 1945, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sought to keep control. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linggadjati-agreement">Linggadjati Agreement</a> of November 1946 both sides agreed to build a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, but the Dutch began creating federal states on their own. A first Pasundan Republic was proclaimed on 4 May 1947 by the Sundanese aristocrat Musa Suriakartalegawa at a gathering of about 5,000 people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a>, to which Dutch military trucks had brought many. It was denounced by Sundanese aristocrats and commoners, and practically disappeared after the Dutch Police Action of July 1947. To the Republican side it confirmed a Dutch intention to divide and rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> administration of the occupied areas then held three conferences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a>. At the last, from 23 February to 5 March 1948, most delegates were pro-Indonesian, and the republican R. A. A. Wiranatakusumah was narrowly elected head of state (wali negara), with Adil Poeradiredja as prime minister. The State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a> came into being on 26 February 1948 and was renamed Pasundan on 24 April. Support for the Republic was so strong in its parliament and cabinet that the Dutch restricted civil liberties, including the right of assembly, and kept control of secondary and higher education, radio and newspapers.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> launched a second Police Action on 19 December 1948 and detained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>, the Pasundan cabinet resigned in protest. Djumhana Wiriaatmadja formed a new one, whose anti-Dutch stance brought threats of military rule; he resigned on 28 January 1949 and formed a cabinet more acceptable to the Dutch. Djumhana later took part in talks with Sukarno on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangka">Bangka</a>, which led to the Round Table Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> and the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> on 27 December 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The state never controlled all its territory: at its founding about a quarter was held by Islamic anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> groups including Darul Islam. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> coup attempt of 23 January 1950, in which several Pasundan leaders were arrested, Wiranatakusumah handed over his powers on 9 February. On 11 March 1950 Pasundan became part of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. In 2009 it was proposed to rename the present province of West Java Pasundan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “State of Pasundan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 27 December 1949 the Netherlands transferred sovereignty to the Republic of the United States of Indonesia, a federation of sixteen states that joined the revolutionary Republic of Indonesia with states set up by the Dutch, such as East Indonesia and <b>Pasundan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-of-indonesia">United States of Indonesia</a></li></ul></section>
